1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,258
You have watched this method work four times against Priya's week.

2
00:00:04,258 --> 00:00:07,218
This is where you find out it works against yours.

3
00:00:07,218 --> 00:00:12,192
Pick something real: not a toy example, the actual task that eats twenty minutes you

4
00:00:12,192 --> 00:00:13,909
would rather spend elsewhere.

5
00:00:13,909 --> 00:00:15,804
It does not need to be dramatic.

6
00:00:15,804 --> 00:00:20,777
A weekly status update you copy from three systems by hand, a folder of receipts you

7
00:00:20,777 --> 00:00:25,573
sort every Friday, an inbox rule you have meant to write for months: any of those

8
00:00:25,573 --> 00:00:26,165
qualifies.

9
00:00:26,165 --> 00:00:29,422
What changes when the task is yours instead of Priya's?

10
00:00:29,422 --> 00:00:31,435
One thing, and it is an advantage.

11
00:00:31,435 --> 00:00:36,290
Following Priya, the course had to hand you her ground truth: what her clients are

12
00:00:36,290 --> 00:00:40,020
like, which email mattered, what the sheet was supposed to say.

13
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:42,566
On your own task, you are the ground truth.

14
00:00:42,566 --> 00:00:47,303
You know which Tuesday email is the dangerous one and what correct looks like on

15
00:00:47,303 --> 00:00:51,744
your desk, which is exactly the independent knowledge Section 7.3 said safe

16
00:00:51,744 --> 00:00:53,046
delegation depends on.

17
00:00:53,046 --> 00:00:56,244
The capstone is not harder than the builds you copied.

18
00:00:56,244 --> 00:01:01,158
It is the first one where the most qualified trial reviewer in the world is already

19
00:01:01,158 --> 00:01:02,461
sitting in your chair.

20
00:01:03,011 --> 00:01:07,700
Every one of those six steps is something you have already done at least once in

21
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:08,404
this course.

22
00:01:08,404 --> 00:01:09,576
Nothing here is new.

23
00:01:09,576 --> 00:01:14,501
What's new is that nobody is handing you Priya's story bible to work from this time.

24
00:01:14,501 --> 00:01:18,194
As a plan, the six steps are smaller than they look on a slide.

25
00:01:18,194 --> 00:01:22,884
One and two are an afternoon: half an hour of writing, then a build you have now

26
00:01:22,884 --> 00:01:23,822
done four times.

27
00:01:23,822 --> 00:01:25,815
Three is an hour with pinned data.

28
00:01:25,815 --> 00:01:29,977
Four runs in the background of your normal week, costing minutes a day.

29
00:01:29,977 --> 00:01:33,084
Five and six are paperwork measured in coffee breaks.

30
00:01:33,084 --> 00:01:37,481
The two places people actually stall are worth naming in advance: step one,

31
00:01:37,481 --> 00:01:42,288
polishing a job description that was good enough forty minutes ago, and step four,

32
00:01:42,288 --> 00:01:47,153
abandoning the trial early because the first outputs looked fine, which Section 7.1

33
00:01:47,153 --> 00:01:48,912
has already armed you against.

34
00:01:48,912 --> 00:01:53,660
Start the afternoon version this week, while the last section's momentum is still

35
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:54,540
yours to spend.

36
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:59,347
The perfect version does not exist, and the shipped version teaches you things the

37
00:01:59,347 --> 00:02:01,047
perfect one never would have.

38
00:02:01,597 --> 00:02:04,944
Do not spend a week searching for the perfect task.

39
00:02:04,944 --> 00:02:10,194
A modest, real task done properly beats an ambitious one left half finished, and

40
00:02:10,194 --> 00:02:13,081
that sentence has been true since Section 1.

41
00:02:13,081 --> 00:02:18,266
If two candidates come to mind, pick whichever one you can describe in a single

42
00:02:18,266 --> 00:02:19,513
sentence right now.

43
00:02:19,513 --> 00:02:23,188
That sentence is the first line of your job description.

44
00:02:23,188 --> 00:02:28,701
The four shapes are also a map back into the course, and using it is encouraged, not

45
00:02:28,701 --> 00:02:29,291
cheating.

46
00:02:29,291 --> 00:02:33,491
A triage-shaped task starts from Section 3 and the C01 template.

47
00:02:33,491 --> 00:02:36,707
An extraction task starts from Section 4 and C02.

48
00:02:36,707 --> 00:02:39,398
A summary on a rhythm, Section 5 and C03.

49
00:02:39,398 --> 00:02:43,007
A chase, Section 6 and C04, draft-only wiring included.

50
00:02:43,007 --> 00:02:47,995
Import the nearest template, strip what your task does not need, rewrite the

51
00:02:47,995 --> 00:02:52,983
briefing in your own words, and you have skipped an hour of plumbing without

52
00:02:52,983 --> 00:02:57,905
skipping a minute of the method, because the method was never the plumbing.

53
00:02:58,455 --> 00:03:03,306
Notice what the finish line does not require: a perfect agent, a finished trial, or

54
00:03:03,306 --> 00:03:04,475
anything at Release.

55
00:03:04,475 --> 00:03:09,092
It requires the method, executed once, with receipts, because the method is the

56
00:03:09,092 --> 00:03:11,839
thing you are actually taking from this course.

57
00:03:11,839 --> 00:03:14,352
The agent is just this month's proof of it.

58
00:03:14,352 --> 00:03:19,145
Next lecture works through a full example from start to finish, so you can see the

59
00:03:19,145 --> 00:03:22,476
whole method run once more before you run it on your own.
